Find the intercepts.
$\begin{align*} x^2-16&=0\\(x-4)(x+4)&=0\\x&=-4,\ 4.\end{align*}$
The curve lies below the $x$-axis between the intercepts.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.6cm,y=0.13cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{areaFill}{HTML}{DCEBE0}
\fill[areaFill] (-4,0) -- plot[domain=-4:4,samples=81] (\x,{\x*\x-16}) -- cycle;
\draw[->,black] (-4.8,0)--(4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-19)--(0,6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-4.5:4.5,samples=91] (\x,{\x*\x-16});
\path (-4,0) node[above left] {$-4$} (4,0) node[above right] {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{align*} A&=-\int_{-4}^4(x^2-16)\,dx\\&=-\left[\frac{x^3}{3}-16x\right]_{-4}^4\\&=-\left(-\frac{128}{3}-\frac{128}{3}\right)\\&=\frac{256}{3}\ \text{units}^2.\end{align*}$

Find the intercepts.
$\begin{align*} x^3-4x&=0\\x(x-2)(x+2)&=0\\x&=-2,\ 0,\ 2.\end{align*}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.85cm,y=0.14cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{areaFill}{HTML}{DCEBE0}
\fill[areaFill] (-2,0) -- plot[domain=-2:0,samples=41] (\x,{\x*\x*\x-4*\x}) -- cycle;
\fill[areaFill] (0,0) -- plot[domain=0:2,samples=41] (\x,{\x*\x*\x-4*\x}) -- cycle;
\fill[areaFill] (2,0) -- plot[domain=2:3,samples=41] (\x,{\x*\x*\x-4*\x}) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->,black] (-2.7,0)--(3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-7)--(0,19) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-2.4:3.1,samples=121] (\x,{\x*\x*\x-4*\x});
\draw[black,dashed] (3,0)--(3,15);
\path (-2,0) node[below left] {$-2$} (0,0) node[below left] {$0$} (2,0) node[above left] {$2$} (3,0) node[below] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A primitive is $F(x)=\frac{x^4}{4}-2x^2$.
$\begin{align*} \int_{-2}^3(x^3-4x)\,dx&=F(3)-F(-2)\\&=\frac94-(-4)\\&=\frac{25}{4}.\end{align*}$
Split at $x=0$ and $x=2$.
$\begin{align*} I_1&=\int_{-2}^0(x^3-4x)\,dx=0-(-4)=4,\\I_2&=\int_0^2(x^3-4x)\,dx=-4-0=-4,\\I_3&=\int_2^3(x^3-4x)\,dx=\frac94-(-4)=\frac{25}{4}.\end{align*}$
$\begin{align*} A&=|I_1|+|I_2|+|I_3|\\&=4+4+\frac{25}{4}\\&=\frac{57}{4}\ \text{units}^2.\end{align*}$
The definite integral subtracts the below-axis area; the total area adds all three magnitudes.

$\begin{align*} x^4-16x^2&=x^2(x-4)(x+4),\\f(-x)&=(-x)^4-16(-x)^2=f(x).\end{align*}$
The function is even. The curve lies below the axis on both halves and touches it at $x=0$ without changing sign.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.6cm,y=0.035cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{areaFill}{HTML}{DCEBE0}
\fill[areaFill] (-4,0) -- plot[domain=-4:4,samples=161] (\x,{\x*\x*\x*\x-16*\x*\x}) -- cycle;
\draw[->,black] (-4.8,0)--(4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-75)--(0,40) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-4.2:4.2,samples=181] (\x,{\x*\x*\x*\x-16*\x*\x});
\path (-4,0) node[above left] {$-4$} (4,0) node[above right] {$4$} (0,0) node[above left] {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{align*} A&=-2\int_0^4(x^4-16x^2)\,dx\\&=-2\left[\frac{x^5}{5}-\frac{16x^3}{3}\right]_0^4\\&=-2\left(\frac{1024}{5}-\frac{1024}{3}\right)\\&=\frac{4096}{15}\ \text{units}^2.\end{align*}$

$\begin{align*} x^3-9x&=x(x-3)(x+3),\\f(-x)&=-x^3+9x=-f(x).\end{align*}$
The intercepts are $x=-3,0,3$. The function is odd, so the two enclosed regions have equal area. On $0<x<3$, the curve lies below the axis.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.17cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{areaFill}{HTML}{DCEBE0}
\fill[areaFill] (-3,0) -- plot[domain=-3:0,samples=61] (\x,{\x*\x*\x-9*\x}) -- cycle;
\fill[areaFill] (0,0) -- plot[domain=0:3,samples=61] (\x,{\x*\x*\x-9*\x}) -- cycle;
\draw[->,black] (-3.8,0)--(3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-13)--(0,13) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.4:3.4,samples=141] (\x,{\x*\x*\x-9*\x});
\path (-3,0) node[above left] {$-3$} (3,0) node[below right] {$3$} (0,0) node[below left] {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{align*} A&=-2\int_0^3(x^3-9x)\,dx\\&=-2\left[\frac{x^4}{4}-\frac{9x^2}{2}\right]_0^3\\&=-2\left(\frac{81}{4}-\frac{81}{2}\right)\\&=\frac{81}{2}\ \text{units}^2.\end{align*}$
